-- title:
--   Single-qubit gates and CNOT are universal
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge1$. The subgroup of $U(2^n)$ generated by all single-qubit gates $U^{(k)}$ ($U\in U(2)$, $k$ a qubit) together with all CNOT gates $\mathrm{CNOT}^{(i,j)}$ on ordered pairs of distinct qubits is the whole unitary group:
--   $$\big\langle\,\mathcal S_n\cup\mathcal G_n(\mathrm{CNOT})\,\big\rangle=U(2^n).$$
--
--   Every $n$-qubit unitary is therefore an exact finite product of single-qubit gates and CNOTs; this is the classical universality theorem of the gate model, here used as the stepping stone to the iSWAP version.
--
--   **Formalization Note** For $n=1$ there are no qubit pairs and the statement reduces to $U(2)$ being generated by single-qubit gates; $n=0$ is excluded because then no generators exist while $U(1)$ is nontrivial.
-- source:
--   Barenco et al., Elementary gates for quantum computation, Phys. Rev. A 52, 3457 (1995), https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevA.52.3457; Nielsen & Chuang §4.5.2; source claim: Wikipedia, "Superconducting quantum computing" (PDF snapshot supplied by the mission author, retrieved 2026-10-13), section 'Criteria for a viable quantum processor'

